'''Shree Cement''' is an Indian [[cement]] manufacturer, founded in [[Beawar]], [[Rajasthan]], in 1979. Headquartered in [[Kolkata]], it is India's third largest cement producer group by capacity<ref name=":3">{{Cite news |date=2023-06-27 |title=Shree Cement cracks, MD says no tax evasion |url=https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/kolkata/shree-cement-cracks-md-says-no-tax-evasion/articleshow/101293309.cms |access-date=2024-01-04 |work=The Times of India |issn=0971-8257}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=2023-12-14 |title=Shree Cement commissions new klin at Rajasthan at ₹3,500 cr |url=https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/companies/shree-cement-commissions-new-klin-at-rajasthan-at-3500-cr/article67638161.ece |access-date=2024-01-04 |website=BusinessLine |language=en}}</ref> and third largest cement company by market capitalisation.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Shree Cement plans to bid in India's lithium auction |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/india/shree-cement-plans-bid-indias-lithium-auction-source-2023-12-06/ |website=Reuters}}</ref> Shree Cement has an installed cement production capacity of 66.8 mtpa including overseas operations.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last=Dutt |first=Ishita Ayan |date=2022-08-09 |title=Shree Cement 'very bullish', setting up new factories: MD Bangur |url=https://www.business-standard.com/article/companies/shree-cement-very-bullish-setting-up-new-factories-ceo-bangur-122080900633_1.html |access-date=2022-09-27 |website=www.business-standard.com |language=en}}</ref> It also produces and sells power under the name Shree Power (captive power plant) and Shree Mega Power.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.forbesindia.com/article/leadership-awards-2017/shree-cements-hari-mohan-bangur-on-solid-ground/48717/1|title=Shree Cement's Hari Mohan Bangur: On solid ground {{!}} Forbes India|website=Forbes India|language=en-US|access-date=2017-12-16}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1= |first1= |date=28 Sep 2014 |title=Concrete Growth |url=http://businesstoday.intoday.in/story/shree-cement-growth-path-consistent-performance-behind-success/1/210086.html |accessdate=19 March 2015 |work=Business Today |publisher= |issue=Sept 2014 |ref=growth |agency=News}}</ref>

== History ==
Shree Cement was incorporated in 1979 by [[Benu Gopal Bangur]]. In 1983, it commissioned its first plant in [[Beawar]], [[Rajasthan]], with production beginning in 1985. In 1995, Bangur's family gained full control of the business. Bangur's son [[Hari Mohan Bangur]], a graduate from [[IIT Bombay]], is the current head of the company. In 2003, Hari Mohan Bangur's son Prashant Bangur also joined the company.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Cementing A Sustainable Future |url=https://www.forbesindia.com/article/take-one-big-story-of-the-day/cementing-a-sustainable-future/75517/1 |access-date=2022-09-27 |website=Forbes India |language=en}}</ref>

In 2014, Shree Cement acquired a grinding unit in [[Panipat]], [[Haryana]].<ref name="BS">{{Cite web |title=Will explore acquisition possibilities at the right price: Shree Cement V-C |url=https://www.business-standard.com/companies/news/will-explore-acquisition-possibilities-at-the-right-price-shree-cement-v-c-123090401024_1.html |website=Business Standard}}</ref>

In 2018, Shree Cement acquired [[UAE]]-based Union Cement, which had a capacity of 4 mtpa in the [[Emirate of Ras Al Khaimah]], for $305 million.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kanungo |first1=Soumonty |title=Shree Cement to buy 93% stake in UAE-based Union Cement for $305 million |url=https://www.livemint.com/Companies/2Bw2GleTzHflT4kIFuLxkO/Shree-Cement-to-buy-93-stake-in-UAEbased-Union-Cement-for.html |access-date=29 August 2024 |work=Mint |date=12 January 2018}}</ref>

In 2019, the company raised {{INR}}2,399 crore through a [[qualified institutional placement]]. In December 2023, it commissioned a [[kiln]] at its plant in [[Nawalgarh, Rajasthan]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Shree Cement commissions high-capacity kiln for ₹3,500 cr Rajasthan plant |url=https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/indl-goods/svs/cement/shree-cement-commissions-high-capacity-kiln-for-3500-cr-rajasthan-plant/articleshow/105941961.cms |website=Economic Times}}</ref>

In 2024, the company entered the [[ready-mix concrete]] (RMC) segment.<ref name="BS1" />

== Operations ==
As of 2025, the company has a production capacity of 62.8 MTPA in India, and 4 MTPA in the United Arab Emirates.<ref name="BS" /><ref name="CWO" /> It operates integrated and grinding units in 18 locations in India.<ref name=":1" /><ref name="CWO">{{Cite web |title=Shree Cement to invest Rs 6000cr to increase capacity |url=https://www.constructionweekonline.in/business/shree-cement-to-invest-rs-6000cr-to-increase-capacity |website=constructionweekonline}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Shree Cement inaugurates cement grinding unit with 3 MTPA capacity in West Bengal - ET RealEstate |url=https://realty.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/cement/shree-cement-inaugurates-cement-grinding-unit-with-3-mtpa-capacity-in-west-bengal/103366621 |access-date=2024-01-04 |website=ETRealty.com |language=en}}</ref>

In 2023, its subsidiary commissioned a new 3 MTPA grinding unit in [[Purulia district]], [[West Bengal]].<ref name=":1" /> In April 2025, the company commissioned a 3 MTPA grinding unit in [[Etah]], [[Uttar Pradesh]]. It also has integrated plants in [[Kodla]], [[Karnataka]], and [[Guntur]], [[Andhra Pradesh]].<ref name="BS1">{{Cite news |title=Shree Cement's strategy of going it alone in the age of consolidation |url=https://www.businesstoday.in/latest/corporate/story/shree-cements-strategy-of-going-it-alone-in-the-age-of-consolidation-431481-2025-05-11 |work=Business Today}}</ref>

===Power generation===
Shree Cement also generates electricity for use in its plants and for sale in the market, with an installed power capacity of 1 GW as of 2025.<ref name=":1" /><ref name="BS1" />

== Controversies ==
In 2018, six workers died at the under-construction Shree Cement factory at [[Kodla]] near [[Sedam]] in [[Kalaburagi district]], [[Karnataka]], after a crane collapsed.<ref>{{cite news |title=Six factory workers killed in a freak accident in Kalaburagi |url=https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/karnataka/six-factory-workers-killed-in-a-freak-accident-in-kalaburagi/article24589525.ece |access-date=29 August 2024 |work=The Hindu |date=3 August 2018 |language=en-IN}}</ref> In 2024, four workers died in separate incidents at the same factory within two months, prompting the [[district collector]] to launch an investigation into the factory's safety protocols.<ref>{{cite news |title=ಕಾರ್ಮಿಕರ ಸರಣಿ ಸಾವು: ಮೃತ್ಯುಕೂಪವಾದ ಸೇಡಂನ ಶ್ರೀ ಸಿಮೆಂಟ್ ಕಾರ್ಖಾನೆ |url=https://tv9kannada.com/karnataka/kalaburagi/serial-death-of-workers-at-shree-cement-factory-in-sedam-kalaburagi-news-in-kannada-krn-871536.html |access-date=29 August 2024 |work=TV9 Kannada |date=24 July 2024 |language=kn}}</ref>

In February 2025, after another worker died at the same factory, a group of workers were filmed dragging his body and dumping it outside the premises. Six workers were arrested under various sections of the [[Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita]] Act.<ref>{{cite news |title=Six arrested for dragging migrant worker’s body in Sedam cement factory |url=https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/karnataka/six-arrested-for-dragging-migrant-workers-body-in-sedam-cement-factory/article69238906.ece |access-date=16 August 2025 |work=The Hindu |date=19 February 2025 |language=en-IN}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=6 arrested after cement factory worker's body dragged on road in Kalaburagi district - Public TV English |url=https://english.publictv.in/6-arrested-after-cement-factory-workers-body-dragged-on-road-in-kalaburagi/ |access-date=16 August 2025 |work=Public TV |date=19 February 2025}}</ref>

